Heat emitted by copper = Heat gained by ice
$m c \Delta \theta=m^{\prime} L\Rightarrow  m^{\prime}=\frac{m c \Delta \theta}{L}\begin{aligned}
& \text { Given, } m=2 \mathrm{~kg}, c=400 \mathrm{~J}^{-\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \mathrm{C}^{-1}} \\
& \Delta \theta=500, L=3.5 \times 10^5 \mathrm{~J}-\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \\
& \therefore \quad m^{\prime}= \frac{2 \times 400 \times 500}{3.5 \times 10^5}=\frac{8}{7} \mathrm{~kg}
\end{aligned}$

The angular distance $(\theta)$ is given by
$\theta=\frac{\lambda}{d}$
Given,
$\begin{aligned}
\theta=2^{\circ} & =\frac{\pi}{180} \times 2, \lambda=6980 Å \\
& =6980 \times 10^{-10} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore & d=\frac{\lambda}{\theta}=\frac{6980 \times 10^{-10} \times 180}{3.14 \times 2} \\
\Rightarrow & d \approx 2 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{~mm}
\end{array}$

Intensity $\left(I=k a^2\right)$, where $a$ is amplitude and $k$ is a constant.
$\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore & I_1=k a_1^2 \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{I_1}{I_2}=\frac{a_1^2}{a_2^2}=\frac{9}{4} \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{a_1}{a_2}=\frac{3}{2}
\end{array}
Maximum intensity
$\begin{aligned}
I_{\max } & =\left(a_1+a_2\right) \\
& =(3+2)=5
\end{aligned}$
Minimum intensity
$I_{\min }=\left(a_1-a_2\right)=(3-2)=1\therefore \frac{I_{\max }}{I_{\min }}=\frac{5}{1}

From Doppler's effect the perceived frequency $f^{\prime}$ is given by
$f^{\prime}=f\left(\frac{v+v_0}{v}\right)$
Given, $f=990 \mathrm{~Hz}, v=300 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}, v_0=33 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}\begin{aligned}
\therefore f^{\prime} & =990\left(\frac{330+33}{330}\right) \\
& =990 \times \frac{363}{330}=1089 \mathrm{~Hz}
\end{aligned}$
